35. ENUM

Questions

1. What is an enum in Java, and why is it used?
-->An enum (Enumeration) in Java is a special data type used to define a fixed set of constant values.

    It is used when a variable should have only a limited number of predefined values, making the code more readable, type-safe,
    and easier to maintain.

  Syntax:
      enum Day {
          MONDAY, TUESDAY, WEDNESDAY, THURSDAY, FRIDAY, SATURDAY, SUNDAY
      }





Example:
      enum Color {
          RED, GREEN, BLUE
      }
      
      public class Main {
          public static void main(String[] args) {
              Color c = Color.RED;
              System.out.println(c);
          }
      }
      
      Output:   RED


Why is enum used?
      Defines a fixed set of constants.
      Improves code readability.
      Provides type safety (prevents invalid values).
      Can have constructors, methods, and fields.

Real-life examples:
      Days of the week (MONDAY, TUESDAY, ...)
      Months (JANUARY, FEBRUARY, ...)
      Traffic signals (RED, YELLOW, GREEN)
      Order status (PENDING, SHIPPED, DELIVERED)


Interview One-Line Answer:
      An enum in Java is a special data type used to represent a fixed set of constants. It improves code readability,
      type safety, and maintainability.




2. Can an enum implement an interface or extend a class?
-->Yes, an enum can implement an interface, but it cannot extend a class.
    Reason:
        Every enum in Java automatically extends java.lang.Enum.
        Since Java does not support multiple inheritance, an enum cannot extend any other class.
        However, an enum can implement one or more interfaces.
          emum is already extending the Enum class so we cannot extend another class if we extend it will
              become multiple inheritance and in java with classes we cannot perform multiple inheritance.
Example: Enum Implementing an Interface

              interface Animal {
                  void sound();
              }
              
              enum Dog implements Animal {
                  LABRADOR, GERMAN_SHEPHERD;
              
                  public void sound() {
                      System.out.println("Bark");
                  }
              }
              
              public class Main {
                  public static void main(String[] args) {
                      Dog.LABRADOR.sound();
                  }
              }

Output : Bark


Key Points :
        ✅ Enum can implement interfaces.
        ❌ Enum cannot extend any class.
        ✅ Every enum implicitly extends java.lang.Enum.

Interview One-Line Answer:
        An enum can implement one or more interfaces, but it cannot extend another class because it already extends java.lang.Enum implicitly.







3. Can an enum declared inside a class have constructors and methods?
-->Yes, an enum declared inside a class can have constructors, methods, and fields, just like a normal class.

        Constructors are used to initialize enum constants.
        Enum constructors are always private (or package-private). You cannot create enum objects using the new keyword.
        Methods can be used to perform operations on enum constants.

Example:
        public class Main {
        
            enum Day {
                MONDAY("Start of the week"),
                FRIDAY("Weekend is near");
        
                private String message;
        
                // Constructor
                Day(String message) {
                    this.message = message;
                }
        
                // Method
                public void display() {
                    System.out.println(message);
                }
            }
        
            public static void main(String[] args) {
                Day.MONDAY.display();
                Day.FRIDAY.display();
            }
        }

Output:
      Start of the week
      Weekend is near


Key Points
      ✅ An enum can have constructors.
      ✅ An enum can have methods.
      ✅ An enum can have fields (variables).
      ❌ Enum constructors cannot be public; they are implicitly private.
      ❌ You cannot create enum objects using new.


Interview One-Line Answer:
      Yes. An enum declared inside a class can have constructors, fields, and methods. Its constructor is used to initialize
            enum constants and cannot be called using the new keyword.






4. Is an enum inside a class implicitly static? Explain.
-->Yes. A nested enum (an enum declared inside a class) is implicitly static.

This means:

You do not need to write the static keyword.
The enum belongs to the class, not to an object (instance) of the class.
You can access it using the class name.
Example:
class Test {

    enum Color {
        RED, GREEN, BLUE
    }
}

public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Test.Color c = Test.Color.RED;
        System.out.println(c);
    }
}

Output:

RED
Explanation:
Color is declared inside the Test class.
Even though static is not written, Java treats it as static.
So, you can access it as Test.Color.RED without creating a Test object.
No Need to Create an Object
Test t = new Test();      // ❌ Not required
Test.Color c = Test.Color.RED;   // ✅ Correct
Key Points
✅ A nested enum is implicitly static.
✅ It can be accessed using the outer class name.
✅ No object of the outer class is needed.
❌ Writing static before an enum is unnecessary because Java adds it automatically.
Interview One-Line Answer:

    Yes. An enum declared inside a class is implicitly static, so it belongs to the class rather than its objects and can be accessed 
    using the class name without creating an object.





5. How can we access a nested enum from another class?
-->A nested enum (an enum declared inside a class) is accessed using the outer class name, followed by the enum name and the enum constant.

Syntax:

OuterClass.EnumName.CONSTANT
Example:
        class Vehicle {
        
            enum Type {
                CAR, BIKE, BUS
            }
        }
        
        public class Main {
            public static void main(String[] args) {
        
                Vehicle.Type t = Vehicle.Type.CAR;
        
                System.out.println(t);
            }
        }
        
        Output: CAR

Explanation:
    Type is an enum inside the Vehicle class.
    Since a nested enum is implicitly static, you don't need to create a Vehicle object.

You can directly access it using:  
      Vehicle.Type.CAR
      Incorrect Way
      Vehicle v = new Vehicle();
      Vehicle.Type t = v.Type.CAR;   // ❌ Compilation Error
      Correct Way
      Vehicle.Type t = Vehicle.Type.CAR;   // ✅ Correct

Key Points :
      ✅ Access a nested enum using OuterClass.EnumName.CONSTANT.
      ✅ No object of the outer class is required.
      ✅ Nested enums are implicitly static.


Interview One-Line Answer:
        A nested enum is accessed using the outer class name followed by the enum name and constant, such as OuterClass.EnumName.CONSTANT, because
        nested enums are implicitly static.





6. From which Java version can we use enum in a switch statement?
-->You can use enum in a switch statement starting from Java 5 (JDK 1.5).

Java 5 introduced both:

Enums
Support for using enums in switch statements
Example:
enum Day {
    MONDAY, TUESDAY, WEDNESDAY
}

public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {

        Day day = Day.TUESDAY;

        switch (day) {
            case MONDAY:
                System.out.println("Start of the week");
                break;

            case TUESDAY:
                System.out.println("Second day");
                break;

            case WEDNESDAY:
                System.out.println("Midweek");
                break;
        }
    }
}

Output: Second day

    Explanation:
          The switch statement compares the enum value (day) with the enum constants.
          You write only the constant name (MONDAY, TUESDAY, etc.), not Day.MONDAY.
    
    Key Points :
          ✅ Enum support was introduced in Java 5 (JDK 1.5).
          ✅ Enums can be used directly in a switch statement.
          ✅ Inside the case labels, use only the enum constant names.
    

Interview One-Line Answer:
      Enums can be used in a switch statement starting from Java 5 (JDK 1.5), when enums were first introduced.





7. What happens if the enum variable in a switch is null?
-->If the enum variable is null, the switch statement throws a NullPointerException (NPE) at runtime.

This happens because the switch tries to determine the enum constant, but the variable does not refer to any enum object.

Example:
enum Day {
    MONDAY, TUESDAY, WEDNESDAY
}

public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {

        Day day = null;

        switch (day) {
            case MONDAY:
                System.out.println("Monday");
                break;

            case TUESDAY:
                System.out.println("Tuesday");
                break;
        }
    }
}

Output : Exception in thread "main" java.lang.NullPointerException


How to Avoid It : Always check whether the enum variable is null before using it in a switch.

if (day != null) {
    switch (day) {
        case MONDAY:
            System.out.println("Monday");
            break;
        case TUESDAY:
            System.out.println("Tuesday");
            break;
    }
} else {
    System.out.println("Day is null");
}

Output : Day is null

Key Points :
      ✅ If the enum variable is null, switch throws a NullPointerException.
      ✅ This is a runtime exception.
      ✅ Check for null before using an enum in a switch.

Interview One-Line Answer:
      If the enum variable in a switch statement is null, Java throws a NullPointerException at runtime.
      To avoid this, check for null before the switch.








8. What method is used to iterate through enum constants?
-->Answer (Simple Interview Style):
      
      The values() method is used to iterate through all enum constants.
      
      values() is a built-in method provided for every enum.
      It returns an array of all enum constants in the order they are declared.

      Example:
            enum Day {
                MONDAY, TUESDAY, WEDNESDAY
            }
            
            public class Main {
                public static void main(String[] args) {
            
                    for (Day day : Day.values()) {
                        System.out.println(day);
                    }
                }
            }
      
      Output:
            MONDAY
            TUESDAY
            WEDNESDAY

Explanation:
      Day.values() returns:
      
      Day[] days = {MONDAY, TUESDAY, WEDNESDAY};
      The enhanced for loop iterates through each enum constant.

Key Points
      ✅ values() returns an array of all enum constants.
      ✅ Constants are returned in the order they are declared.
      ✅ It is commonly used with a for-each loop.

Interview One-Line Answer:
      The values() method is used to iterate through enum constants. It returns an array containing all enum constants 
      in the order they are declared.




9. What does the ordinal() method return for enum constants?
-->The ordinal() method returns the position (index) of an enum constant, starting from 0.

    The first enum constant has an ordinal value of 0.
    The second has 1.
    The third has 2, and so on.

Example:
      enum Day {
          MONDAY, TUESDAY, WEDNESDAY
      }
      
      public class Main {
          public static void main(String[] args) {
      
              System.out.println(Day.MONDAY.ordinal());
              System.out.println(Day.TUESDAY.ordinal());
              System.out.println(Day.WEDNESDAY.ordinal());
          }
      }

Output:
      0
      1
      2

Example Using values() and ordinal()  :
        enum Day {
            MONDAY, TUESDAY, WEDNESDAY
        }
        
        public class Main {
            public static void main(String[] args) {
        
                for (Day day : Day.values()) {
                    System.out.println(day + " : " + day.ordinal());
                }
            }
        }
        
        Output:
            MONDAY : 0
            TUESDAY : 1
            WEDNESDAY : 2

Key Points :
      ✅ ordinal() returns the index of an enum constant.
      ✅ Indexing starts from 0.
      ✅ The value depends on the order in which the constants are declared.
      ⚠️ Avoid using ordinal() for business logic because changing the order of enum constants changes their ordinal values.


Interview One-Line Answer:
      The ordinal() method returns the zero-based position (index) of an enum constant in the order it is declared.







10. What is EnumSet and EnumMap? Why are they preferred for enum iteration?
-->1. EnumSet

EnumSet is a special Set implementation designed only for enum types.

It stores unique enum constants.
It is very fast and memory-efficient compared to HashSet.

Example:

import java.util.EnumSet;

enum Day {
    MONDAY, TUESDAY, WEDNESDAY, THURSDAY
}

public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {

        EnumSet<Day> days = EnumSet.of(Day.MONDAY, Day.WEDNESDAY);

        for (Day day : days) {
            System.out.println(day);
        }
    }
}

Output:

MONDAY
WEDNESDAY
2. EnumMap

EnumMap is a special Map implementation where the keys are enum constants.

It is faster and uses less memory than HashMap.
Keys must be of the same enum type.

Example:

import java.util.EnumMap;

enum Day {
    MONDAY, TUESDAY, WEDNESDAY
}

public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {

        EnumMap<Day, String> map = new EnumMap<>(Day.class);

        map.put(Day.MONDAY, "Start");
        map.put(Day.TUESDAY, "Work");
        map.put(Day.WEDNESDAY, "Middle");

        for (Day day : map.keySet()) {
            System.out.println(day + " : " + map.get(day));
        }
    }
}

Output:
    MONDAY : Start
    TUESDAY : Work
    WEDNESDAY : Middle

Why are EnumSet and EnumMap preferred?
      ✅ Optimized specifically for enum types.
      ✅ Faster than HashSet and HashMap.
      ✅ Use less memory.
      ✅ Maintain the natural order of enum constants.
      ✅ Provide better performance when working with enums.
Key Points
      ✅ EnumSet is a Set for enum values.
      ✅ EnumMap is a Map with enum keys.
      ✅ Both are optimized for speed and memory.
      ✅ They are preferred over HashSet and HashMap when working with enums.



Interview One-Line Answer:
      EnumSet is a specialized Set for enum constants, and EnumMap is a specialized Map with enum keys. They are preferred because 
      they are faster, memory-efficient, and optimized specifically for enum types.




